@@text Entry fields: ID, feature, target state, world group, commands, expected log, pass / fail, if it fails.
@@text
@@text **Perf session (every bulk run, added 2026-10-28; [../perf.md](../perf.md) 14).** Every `scratch_server.sh` server now
@@text records JFR (`CE_PERF=0` at setup turns it off). Before the first boot run `bash tools/perf/session/perf_session.sh start`;
@@text before each entry run `perf_session.sh mark <ID>` (scratch_server.sh logs boot / cmd / stop itself); after the last
@@text server has stopped run `perf_session.sh stop`, then `perf_session.sh report` (no server up while it parses; no `--prune`, the raw recordings are kept for debugging since 2026-10-31).
@@text `perf/sessions/<name>/summary.md` gives each boot's launch-to-Done time and each entry's tick median / p95 / ticks over
@@text 50 ms / CPU / chunk waits, plus the worst ticks with the commands before them. Add a line to the Run log naming the
@@text session. Disk: `/` is nearly full; one boot's recording is capped at `JFR_MAX` (1g) and `--prune` deletes every raw file, so do not use it; when space runs out, delete by hand the recordings of boots whose rows all passed.
@@text Benchmarks (boot, idle, pregen, reload) are the perf suite's G scenarios ([../perf_suite.md](../perf_suite.md), its own
@@text runner), not queue rows.
